segments.text:
Thomas, when he had ſéene the gaſhes where the nailes entred, & the open wound of Chriſt his ſide, he ſpake foorth alowd; My Lord & my God.
Alſo Chriſt praied to his father ſaieng; Glorifie me, ò Father, with the glorie,
which I had with thee, before the world was made: which ſaieng might not ſtand, vnleſſe that Chriſt had the diuine nature; for his humane nature was not before the world was made. Alſo the Lord ſaid;
All things that my father hath, are mine: and that the father hath the diuine nature, it is by none called in queſtion; and ſo of neceſſitie the ſonne is not without the ſame. Beſides, Chriſt teſtifieth & ſaith; All things that my father dooth,
I alſo doo: but the action of them both, being all one, the natures of them muſt néeds be one and the ſame. He ſaid to the Iewes;
Before that Abraham was, I am: which cannot otherwiſe be referred but to his diuine nature: and no leſſe do theſe ſaiengs prooue; I am the reſurrection and the life.
Againe; As the father hath life in himſelfe, ſo he hath granted to the ſonne to haue life in himſelf.
Hereby it is prooued, that the ſonne is equall to the father. And the ſame Iohn wrote in his firſt epiſtle, the fift chapter;
And we are in him that is true: that is, in his ſonne Chriſt, which is verie God, and eternall life. Our ſauiour ſaid to
Nichodemus;
No man aſcendeth into heauen, but he that came downe from heauen, euen the ſon of man, which is in heauen. And Chriſt was not in heauen, while he talked with the Iewes, vnleſſe it were as touching his diuine nature: neither did he deſcend out of heauen, but in reſpect that he was God. Of which comming downe, he againe teſtifieth,
when he ſaith;
I came downe from heauen, not to doo mine owne will, but the will of him that ſent me.
Againe, I came out from my father, and I go vnto my father.

5 All theſe things muſt be referred vnto the diuine nature. In the Apocalypſe alſo he teſtifieth, that he is α and ω,
The firſt and the laſt. And in the ſame booke, the 19. chapter, it is written of Chriſt, that there was written vpon his thigh, and vpon his garments,
King of kings, and Lord of lords. And leaſt thou ſhouldſt imagine, that that litle, was the
Epitheton aſcribed vnto God, there is mention made in the ſeauentéenth chapter of the lambe himſelfe; that He had the victorie,
bicauſe he was the King of kings, and Lord of lords. Which propertie, that it belongs onlie vnto God, it is manifeſt by the firſt epiſtle of Paule vnto Timothie, where, towards the end of the epiſtle, it is written;
Vntill the appearing of our Lord Ieſus,
which he ſhall ſhew in due time, that is bleſſed, and Prince onlie, the King of kings, and Lord of lords. To the Romans he ſaith;
Of them came the fathers, of whome, as touching the fleſh, Chriſt came, who is God ouer all, bleſſed for euer. Beſides, when it is commanded;
Thou ſhalt woorſhip the Lord thy God, and him onlie thou ſhalt ſerue. It is ſaid by Paule vnto the Philippians; In the name of Ieſus,
let euerie knee bow, both of things in heauen, of things in earth, and things vnder the earth. And to the Hebrues; When he bringeth his ſonne into the world,
he ſaith, Let all the angels of God woorſhip him. And in the ſame place,
out of the ſentence of the pſalme, the power of creating is attributed vnto him;
And thou Lord in the beginning, didſt laie the foundation of the earth. There is alſo another pſalme alledged; Therefore hath God, euen thy God annointed thee with oile of gladnes aboue thy fellowes.

And there is a compariſon of Chriſt with
Melchiſedecke, namelie, that they were both without father,
without mother, and without genealogie; which thing accordeth not with Chriſt, but ſo far foorth as he is God. Vnto the Coloſſinas we reade;
that In Chriſt dwelleth all the fulnes of the godhead bodilie.
Againe; All things are made by him, whether they be things viſible or inuiſible,
or thrones, or dominions. Vnto Titus; We expecting the bleſſed hope, and comming of the glorie of the great God. Héere Chriſt is moſt plainelie called The great God. And in the ſecond to the Corinthians;
When he was rich, he was made poore for all men. It can not be meant that he was rich, but in reſpect of his diuine nature.
And in the firſt epiſtle to the Corinthians; If they had knowne him, they would neuer haue crucified the Lord of glorie. And in the eight chapter;
To vs neuertheles there is one God, which is the father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Ieſus Chriſt, by whome are all things, and we by him. But if that all things are by him, verelie there is no doubt, but that he is God. And vnto the Galathians, as touching the time of infidelitie, it is written;
When as yee ſerued thoſe which in nature be no gods, &c. By which words, the contrarie is gathered; that ſéeing they were conuerted vnto Chriſt, and ſerued him, they ſerued him that in nature is God.
